{"language":"FCL-1","records":[{"id":"sc1","type":"claim","text":"The account's core diagnosis (under-specified agreements; allocation decaying against changing schedules) survives these objections; what is contested is order, a hidden disjunction, an internal tension, one overconfident assertion, and an omission.","scope":"scope-setting for this objection set","mentions":["h005.fork5.p.objection.0#c1","h005.fork5.p.objection.0#c2"]},{"id":"ob1","type":"objection","text":"The ordered plan performs system design (meeting, written record, allocation mechanism) before the one-on-one conversation, although the account's own conjecture includes branches (feeling out-voted; rigged process) under which a design meeting the avoidant friend skips, followed by a system built without them, corroborates and entrenches the grievance.","target":["h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a1","h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a4","h005.fork5.p.objection.0#c3"],"grounds":"The task confirms only that the friend avoids the conversations, not why; on the out-voted and rigged-process branches the current order is costly while the reversed order is nearly free.","bearing":"If this holds, the private conversation should precede the meeting; the objection concerns sequencing, not the content of any single step."},{"id":"ob2","type":"objection","text":"u1 assigns p1 (what the friend is avoiding) to the house meeting as a question 'to answer there', but on the branches where the answer matters most, the meeting is the one venue where the friend will not disclose it; the answer is reachable, if at all, only through the private conversation.","target":["h005.fork5.p.objection.0#u1","h005.fork5.p.objection.0#p1"],"bearing":"The meeting agenda should not assume p1 is answerable in plenary; diagnosis of p1 must run through a4 or not at all."},{"id":"ob3","type":"objection","text":"a3 presents a weekly re-allocation and trigger-based rules as interchangeable, but under changing schedules a trigger such as first-one-home systematically loads the same person, creates perverse marginal incentives, and adds a contestable fact (who arrived when) in a household already disputing what agreements meant; the weekly re-deal keeps drift visible and correctable while triggers automate it.","target":["h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a3"],"mentions":["h005.fork5.p.objection.0#c2"],"bearing":"Triggers should be dropped or adopted only with rotation or caps; the disjunction should not be presented as risk-equivalent."},{"id":"ob4","type":"objection","text":"a2 (checkable specificity) and o1's guard (minimal, revisable record) are opposite pulls with no stated criterion for where they meet, and they diverge most on the account's own re-litigation branch, where minimal records are gameable through their gaps; the claim that written specificity helps in both cases is asserted rather than shown.","target":["h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a2","h005.fork5.p.objection.0#o1"],"bearing":"Repairable from inside the account: designate the weekly re-deal as the standing venue where wording disputes are settled, reducing the burden the record must carry."},{"id":"ob5","type":"objection","text":"The claim that avoidant people often engage readily in writing is asserted without support in the material, and the async channel is double-edged: it can lower the cost of avoidance (skim, never reply, with the record then documenting silence) as easily as the cost of engagement; a4 also carries an unflagged meta-risk that a one-on-one about why someone avoids chore-talks is itself a chore-talk at one remove.","target":["h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a4"],"bearing":"Not grounds to drop a4; grounds to hold it loosely and pre-plan for non-response, which the account does not do."},{"id":"ob6","type":"objection","text":"The plan has no fallback: every remedy except the private conversation requires the participation whose absence is itself one of the presented problems, and nothing is offered for the case where the friend will not attend or reply; shrinking the commons (own dishes, personal zones, permanent single-owner domains) reduces the number of agreements needed, is partly unilateral, and is compatible with the whole proposal.","target":["h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a1","h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a2","h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a3","h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a5","h005.fork5.p.objection.0#u1"],"bearing":"An omission claim rather than a refutation: a commons-shrinking option should be added as the plan's floor."},{"id":"pr1","type":"problem","text":"The material does not reveal whether the user already knows why the friend avoids the talks; if they do, ob1 and ob2 largely dissolve into emphasis rather than correction.","mentions":["h005.fork5.p.objection.0#p1"]},{"id":"use1","type":"use","text":"Take up the account's plan with four amendments: run the private conversation first and plan for silence; prefer the weekly re-deal over triggers, or cap the triggers; make the re-deal the standing venue where disputes about what records mean are settled; add a commons-shrinking fallback as the minimum.","target":["ob1","ob2","ob3","ob4","ob6"],"mentions":["h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a1","h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a2","h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a3","h005.fork5.p.objection.0#a4"]}],"uptake":["ob1","ob2","ob3","ob4","ob5","ob6","use1"]}